implementationPlan.description:
Tests first (load test-driven-development). Create packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/diskStateDispatch.test.ts with your session's file-editing tool, never a shell write (rail). Use exactly the content below.

Why a new file: the existing claimNextTask and releaseTask suites call the handlers directly, so they never pass through McpAdapter's dispatch wrap, which is where the bug lives. This file drives both tools through `McpAdapter.handle`:
- The mocked capture records `state.mutex.isHeld()`, then parks on a gate the test opens.
- While it is parked, a bystander `moe.list_tasks` must answer (DoD 1).
- Spies on `updateTask`/`updateWorker` prove every claim and release write still runs under the tool's own `state.runExclusive` (DoD 2).
- Under the bug the bystander queues behind the parked capture. The 2 s bound then opens the gate, so the case fails on its assertions instead of hanging, well inside vitest's 5 s default timeout.
Keep exactly these two cases and their `(d1) ` / `(d2) ` title prefixes: the RED checkRef pins `2 failed (2)` and both prefixes.

```ts
// task-5158e85b: claim_next_task's post-claim recompute and release_task's
// handoff capture spawn `git status` (util/diskState.ts, killed at 3 s). Both
// tools are `blocking`, so MCP dispatch (McpAdapter) does not wrap them in the
// global state mutex: the git call runs with the mutex free and other tools keep
// answering, while each tool's own writes still run inside its state.runExclusive.
import { afterEach, beforeEach, describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';
import { McpAdapter, type JsonRpcResponse } from '../server/McpAdapter.js';
import { ToolTestHarness } from './toolTestHarness.js';
import { computeDiskStateSignature } from '../util/diskState.js';

vi.mock('../util/diskState.js', () => ({ computeDiskStateSignature: vi.fn() }));
const mockedSignature = vi.mocked(computeDiskStateSignature);

const STORED = 'v1:9f2c0a1b3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6d7e8f9012345:3:0a1b2c3d4e5f';
const MOVED = 'v1:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa:1:ffeeddccbbaa';
// With the mutex free the bystander answers in milliseconds. Under the bug it
// queues behind the parked capture, so the test opens the gate after this bound
// and fails on its assertions instead of hanging.
const BYSTANDER_BOUND_MS = 2000;

describe('disk-state git call vs the fleet-wide state mutex (MCP dispatch)', () => {
  const h = new ToolTestHarness();
  let adapter: McpAdapter;
  let nextId = 1;
  let heldDuringCapture: boolean[];
  let heldDuringWrites: boolean[];
  let events: string[];
  let captureEntered: Promise<void>;
  let finishCapture: () => void;

  beforeEach(() => {
    h.init();
    h.setupMoeFolder();
    h.createEpic();
    heldDuringCapture = [];
    heldDuringWrites = [];
    events = [];
    let entered!: () => void;
    captureEntered = new Promise<void>((resolve) => { entered = resolve; });
    const gate = new Promise<void>((resolve) => { finishCapture = resolve; });
    mockedSignature.mockReset();
    mockedSignature.mockImplementation(async () => {
      heldDuringCapture.push(h.state.mutex.isHeld());
      entered();
      await gate;
      events.push('capture-end');
      return MOVED;
    });
  });

  afterEach(() => {
    finishCapture(); // never leave a capture parked on the gate
    vi.restoreAllMocks();
    h.cleanup();
  });

  /** Load state, record whether each task/worker write runs under the mutex, then build the adapter. */
  async function start(): Promise<void> {
    await h.state.load();
    const realUpdateTask = h.state.updateTask.bind(h.state);
    vi.spyOn(h.state, 'updateTask').mockImplementation(async (...args) => {
      heldDuringWrites.push(h.state.mutex.isHeld());
      return realUpdateTask(...args);
    });
    const realUpdateWorker = h.state.updateWorker.bind(h.state);
    vi.spyOn(h.state, 'updateWorker').mockImplementation(async (...args) => {
      heldDuringWrites.push(h.state.mutex.isHeld());
      return realUpdateWorker(...args);
    });
    adapter = new McpAdapter(h.state);
  }

  function call(name: string, args: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<JsonRpcResponse> {
    return adapter.handle({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: nextId++, method: 'tools/call', params: { name, arguments: args } }) as Promise<JsonRpcResponse>;
  }

  function payload(response: JsonRpcResponse): Record<string, unknown> {
    expect(response.error).toBeUndefined();
    const result = response.result as { content: Array<{ text: string }> };
    return JSON.parse(result.content[0].text) as Record<string, unknown>;
  }

  /** Once the capture is parked, send moe.list_tasks and report whether it answered within the bound. */
  async function bystanderDuringCapture(): Promise<{ winner: string; listed: Promise<JsonRpcResponse> }> {
    await captureEntered;
    const listed = call('moe.list_tasks', {}).then((response) => { events.push('bystander'); return response; });
    const winner = await Promise.race([
      listed.then(() => 'bystander'),
      new Promise<string>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve('bound'), BYSTANDER_BOUND_MS)),
    ]);
    return { winner, listed };
  }

  it('(d1) release_task with a handoffNote captures disk state with the mutex free while a bystander tool answers', async () => {
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-1', status: 'WORKING', assignedWorkerId: 'worker-a' });
    h.createWorker({ id: 'worker-a', currentTaskId: 'task-1', status: 'CODING' });
    await start();

    const release = call('moe.release_task', {
      taskId: 'task-1', workerId: 'worker-a', handoffNote: { whatIsDone: 'half', whatRemains: 'the rest' },
    });
    const { winner, listed } = await bystanderDuringCapture();
    finishCapture();
    const [released, bystander] = await Promise.all([release, listed]);

    expect(heldDuringCapture).toEqual([false]);
    expect(winner).toBe('bystander');
    expect(events).toEqual(['bystander', 'capture-end']);
    payload(bystander);
    expect(payload(released)).toMatchObject({ success: true, diskStateCaptured: true });
    expect(h.state.getTask('task-1')!.assignedWorkerId).toBeNull();
    expect(h.state.getTask('task-1')!.priorHandoffs![0].diskState).toBe(MOVED);
    expect(heldDuringWrites.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    expect(heldDuringWrites.every(Boolean)).toBe(true);
  });

  it('(d2) claim_next_task recomputes a stored handoff diskState with the mutex free while a bystander tool answers', async () => {
    h.createTask({
      id: 'task-2', status: 'WORKING', assignedWorkerId: null,
      priorHandoffs: [{ whatIsDone: 'x', whatRemains: 'y', releasedBy: 'worker-gone', releasedAt: new Date().toISOString(), diskState: STORED }],
    });
    h.createWorker({ id: 'worker-b' });
    await start();

    const claim = call('moe.claim_next_task', { workerId: 'worker-b', statuses: ['WORKING'] });
    const { winner, listed } = await bystanderDuringCapture();
    finishCapture();
    const [claimed, bystander] = await Promise.all([claim, listed]);

    expect(heldDuringCapture).toEqual([false]);
    expect(winner).toBe('bystander');
    expect(events).toEqual(['bystander', 'capture-end']);
    payload(bystander);
    expect(payload(claimed)).toMatchObject({ hasNext: true, staleHandoffDiskState: true });
    expect(h.state.getTask('task-2')!.assignedWorkerId).toBe('worker-b');
    expect(heldDuringWrites.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    expect(heldDuringWrites.every(Boolean)).toBe(true);
  });
});
```

Then run the RED checkRef (the execution contract's second) verbatim, once. It archives base 09529122, where claimNextTask.ts, releaseTask.ts and McpAdapter.ts are byte-identical to the hunt's f159784e, and overlays only this file. It must print both FAIL lines, `Tests  2 failed (2)` and `RED-AT-BASE`. Each case fails on `expected [ true ] to deeply equal [ false ]`: the capture ran under the mutex, which is the bug.

Never run npm or vitest in the checkout or your worktree (rail). Both checkRefs build their own mktemp snapshot.

Architect dry run (11-04): RED-AT-BASE at 09529122. With step 2 applied the file passes 2/2 in about 0.6 s. Removing `blocking` from release_task alone kills only (d1); removing it from claim_next_task alone kills only (d2). The file typechecks under the GREEN checkRef's tt.json.

Fix (load ponytail; read mem:gotcha-daemon-global-mutex-dispatch). Mark both tools `blocking: true`, following the submit_plan precedent (tools/submitPlan.ts:198), so McpAdapter.ts:309 stops wrapping their whole handler in the global state mutex. Use your file-editing tool, never a shell write (rail).

(a) packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/claimNextTask.ts, in claimNextTaskTool's returned object, directly after `    name: 'moe.claim_next_task',` insert:
```ts
    // Mutex opt-out (tools/index.ts ToolDefinition.blocking): once the claim's
    // own state.runExclusive below releases, the stale-handoff recompute spawns
    // `git status` (util/diskState.ts, killed at 3 s); a dispatch-level wrap
    // would hold the fleet-wide mutex across it. Every read and write of the
    // claim stays inside that runExclusive.
    blocking: true,
```
(b) packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/releaseTask.ts, in releaseTaskTool's returned object, directly after `    name: 'moe.release_task',` insert:
```ts
    // Mutex opt-out (tools/index.ts ToolDefinition.blocking): a handoffNote's
    // disk-state capture spawns `git status` (util/diskState.ts, killed at 3 s)
    // before the handler's state.runExclusive; a dispatch-level wrap would hold
    // the fleet-wide mutex across it. The ownership gates and every write stay
    // inside that runExclusive; before it the handler only validates input,
    // checks the caller's role for force and captures.
    blocking: true,
```
Change nothing else in either file.

Why this is safe (verified at 09529122):
- claim_next_task's handler (:421) awaits `state.runExclusive` (:429) with no await before it. It still takes the lock synchronously on entry, so claim ordering is unchanged. After the block it only recomputes the signature (:1019) and builds the reply. Never add an await before that runExclusive (mem:gotcha-submit-plan-pre-mutex-await-ordering).
- Before its `state.runExclusive` (:194), release_task's handler only validates input, reads the caller's role for force (`workerHasRole`, a synchronous map read), clamps the note and captures. That runExclusive re-reads the row and runs every ownership gate and write. A release without a handoffNote still reaches the lock synchronously.
- Never move computeDiskStateSignature into a runExclusive callback (util/diskState.ts invariant 2).

Two constraints:
- The comments must not match daemonGitDocs.test.ts's /never (?:runs|executes|touches) git|runs no git|does not run git/i. It scans every non-test daemon source line, and it is in the gate.
- Do not touch moe-proxy's BLOCKING_LONG_POLLS (packages/moe-proxy/src/index.ts:273). It is the proxy's long-poll timeout list, not this flag. Both tools keep the 30 s request timeout, and the capture is killed at 3 s.

Name both tools wherever the daemon documents the blocking set (DoD 2). Use your file-editing tool, never a shell write (rail). Each anchor below occurs exactly once at 09529122.

(a) packages/moe-daemon/src/server/McpAdapter.ts: the tools/call dispatch comment above `const invoke` (~:300). Replace these three lines:
```
          // Blocking tools opt out — they'd hold the lock for minutes. The
          // mutex is reentrant, so tools that already call runExclusive
          // internally (claim_next_task, submit_plan, …) are safe to wrap.
```
with:
```
          // `blocking` tools opt out and take state.runExclusive themselves
          // around every read-modify-write: wait_for_task, chat_wait and
          // wait_for_resource park for minutes, while claim_next_task and
          // release_task (the handoff disk-state `git status`,
          // util/diskState.ts), submit_plan (its cached-ref Git lookup) and
          // get_execution_readiness (a bounded evaluation) do slow work
          // outside their own exclusive section that a wrap here would make
          // every other tool wait on. The mutex is reentrant, so a wrapped
          // tool that also calls runExclusive internally does not deadlock.
```
This is a comment-only change. Do not touch the env/RateLimiter lines near the top (~:15-56): open row task-301cf760 owns them.

(b) packages/moe-daemon/CLAUDE.md, first bullet. Replace this sentence:
```text
`submit_plan` also opts out for its bounded cached-Git lookup, serializing and revalidating each write attempt itself.
```
with:
```text
Tools that do bounded slow work outside their own `state.runExclusive` opt out too and serialize every write themselves: `submit_plan` (its cached-Git lookup; it revalidates each write attempt), `claim_next_task` and `release_task` (the handoff disk-state `git status` in `util/diskState.ts`, up to 3 s) and `get_execution_readiness` (a bounded evaluation).
```
The daemonGitDocs phrases are forbidden here too. The GREEN checkRef overlays the edited CLAUDE.md and runs that test.

